---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <BaseHead
    title="DSCR Loans for Real Estate Investors | Rental Property Financing | Broker Pal"
    description="Learn how DSCR rental property loans work, compare investor financing advantages, understand financed-property limits, and calculate estimated rent coverage with Broker Pal."
  />
  <meta name="keywords" content="DSCR loans, DSCR loan for investors, rental property financing, investment property loans, DSCR calculator, how many properties can I finance, LLC rental property loan, cash out refinance investment property" />
</head>
<body>
  <Header />
  <main class="investor-page">
    <section class="investor-hero">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <div class="kicker">THE BROKER PAL INVESTOR RESOURCE</div>
        <h1>Build your rental portfolio with <em>financing that understands the property.</em></h1>
        <p>Explore how Debt Service Coverage Ratio (DSCR) loans work, what lenders look for, and how rental income may help support your next investment-property purchase or refinance.</p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button primary" href="/apply">Explore Your DSCR Options <span>→</span></a>
          <a class="button secondary" href="#dscr-calculator">Calculate Your DSCR <span>↓</span></a>
        </div>
        <small>Investor-purpose financing · Program availability and terms vary by lender</small>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-stat" aria-label="DSCR example">
        <span>AN EXAMPLE OF RENT COVERAGE</span>
        <strong>1.25<small>×</small></strong>
        <b>$2,500 qualifying monthly rent</b>
        <i>÷</i>
        <b>$2,000 monthly PITIA</b>
        <p>Illustrative DSCR calculation</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="intro-section">
      <div class="section-label">WHAT IS A DSCR LOAN?</div>
      <h2>Let the property's income help tell the story.</h2>
      <p>A DSCR loan is an investment-property mortgage that evaluates the property's qualifying rental income in relation to its housing expense. Instead of relying primarily on the borrower's personal debt-to-income calculation, the lender focuses heavily on whether the property's rent supports the proposed payment.</p>
      <p>That can be useful for real estate investors whose tax returns, business income, depreciation, or growing portfolio make traditional income documentation less straightforward. Lenders may still review credit, assets, reserves, experience, guarantors, and other factors.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="benefits-section">
      <div class="section-label">WHY INVESTORS CONSIDER DSCR</div>
      <h2>Potential advantages of DSCR rental financing</h2>
      <div class="benefit-grid">
        <article><span>01</span><h3>Property cash flow focus</h3><p>Qualification places substantial weight on the subject property's eligible rental income and housing expense.</p></article>
        <article><span>02</span><h3>Less reliance on personal income</h3><p>Many DSCR programs do not use traditional personal-income qualification in the same way as conventional mortgages. Other borrower reviews may still apply.</p></article>
        <article><span>03</span><h3>Portfolio-minded options</h3><p>Some programs allow investors to finance multiple rentals, with limits based on property count, total exposure, reserves, and program rules.</p></article>
        <article><span>04</span><h3>Purchase or refinance strategies</h3><p>Depending on the program, investors may use DSCR financing for purchases, rate-and-term refinances, or cash-out refinances.</p></article>
        <article><span>05</span><h3>Potential entity vesting</h3><p>Some programs permit eligible LLC or other entity vesting. Ownership, guaranty, and documentation rules vary by lender and state.</p></article>
        <article><span>06</span><h3>Options for different rental plans</h3><p>Programs may consider long-term rentals and, where specifically allowed, short-term or seasonal rental income.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="fine-print">These are potential program features, not promises that every loan includes every option. Credit, property type, occupancy, loan size, leverage, reserves, state law, and lender guidelines affect eligibility.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="calculator-section" id="dscr-calculator">
      <div class="calculator-copy">
        <div class="section-label">TRY THE NUMBERS</div>
        <h2>Estimate your rental property's DSCR.</h2>
        <p>Enter estimated qualifying monthly rent and monthly PITIA (principal, interest, taxes, insurance, and association dues, if applicable). This quick estimate is for planning—not an underwriting decision.</p>
        <div class="calc-note"><b>Formula</b><span>Qualifying monthly rent ÷ monthly PITIA = estimated DSCR</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="calculator-card">
        <label for="monthly-rent">Estimated qualifying monthly rent ($)</label>
        <input id="monthly-rent" type="number" min="0" step="50" value="2500" inputmode="decimal" />
        <label for="monthly-pitia">Estimated monthly PITIA ($)</label>
        <input id="monthly-pitia" type="number" min="1" step="50" value="2000" inputmode="decimal" />
        <div class="calc-result"><span>ESTIMATED DSCR</span><strong id="dscr-result">1.25×</strong><small id="dscr-explanation">$1.25 of qualifying rent for each $1.00 of PITIA.</small></div>
        <p class="fine-print">Actual lender calculations may use different rent documentation, payment components, vacancy factors, or minimum DSCR requirements. This estimate does not include every operating expense such as repairs, utilities, management, or vacancy.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="property-count-section">
      <div class="section-label">GROWING YOUR PORTFOLIO</div>
      <h2>How many financed properties can an investor have?</h2>
      <div class="count-layout">
        <div class="count-highlight"><strong>10</strong><span>Fannie Mae Desktop Underwriter (DU) limit for second-home and investment-property transactions under its multiple-financed-property policy.</span></div>
        <div>
          <p>That conventional guideline is often confused with DSCR loan limits. It is not a universal legal cap on how many rental properties an investor may own or finance through DSCR programs.</p>
          <p>DSCR property-count limits are set by individual lenders and programs. Some cap the number of financed properties or loans; others may allow more than ten while applying total borrower-exposure limits, minimum reserves, guarantor requirements, or other restrictions.</p>
          <p><b>The practical question is not only “How many properties?”</b> It is also how the lender measures aggregate loan balances, properties owned, loans in process, liquidity, and guarantor exposure. We can help review those details against available program guidelines.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b2-2-03/multiple-financed-properties-same-borrower" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Review Fannie Mae's multiple financed properties guidance ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="fine-print">Fannie Mae's limit applies to its own conventional underwriting policy and eligible transaction types. DSCR lender rules are separate and vary. Always confirm the current guideline for the specific program.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="facts-section">
      <div class="section-label">INVESTOR QUICK FACTS</div>
      <h2>A few useful things to know before you make an offer.</h2>
      <div class="facts-grid">
        <article><b>1.00×</b><h3>Break-even coverage example</h3><p>A 1.00× ratio means qualifying rent equals the PITIA amount used in the calculation.</p></article>
        <article><b>1.25×</b><h3>More rent than PITIA</h3><p>A 1.25× ratio means $1.25 in qualifying monthly rent for every $1.00 of monthly PITIA.</p></article>
        <article><b>Rent matters</b><h3>Documentation can vary</h3><p>Lenders may use an existing lease, appraiser rent schedule, or other program-approved rental evidence.</p></article>
        <article><b>Not net profit</b><h3>DSCR is not cash in your pocket</h3><p>A simple rent-to-PITIA ratio does not subtract every operating cost, vacancy, repair, or capital expense.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="process-section">
      <div class="section-label">WHAT LENDERS MAY REVIEW</div>
      <h2>Look beyond the ratio.</h2>
      <div class="process-grid">
        <div><b>Property</b><p>Eligible property type, condition, appraisal, location, occupancy, and market rent support.</p></div>
        <div><b>Borrower</b><p>Credit profile, liquidity, reserves, experience, entity structure, and guarantor requirements.</p></div>
        <div><b>Loan terms</b><p>Loan amount, LTV, interest rate, amortization, prepayment terms, and total lender exposure.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section">
      <div class="section-label">DSCR LOAN FAQ</div>
      <h2>Common questions from rental property investors</h2>
      <details><summary>Do I need to provide personal tax returns for a DSCR loan?</summary><p>Many DSCR programs focus on the subject property's qualifying rental income rather than using personal tax-return income as the primary qualification method. A lender may still request other personal, business, asset, credit, or entity documentation.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does a DSCR loan require a 1.00 ratio?</summary><p>Not every program has the same minimum. Some require a ratio at or above 1.00×, while others may consider lower ratios with different pricing, leverage, credit, or reserve requirements. Program rules change, so the specific guideline must be checked.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I close a DSCR loan in an LLC?</summary><p>Some DSCR programs permit eligible entities such as LLCs, often with personal guarantor requirements. Entity eligibility, ownership structure, and documentation vary by lender and state.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I use short-term rental income?</summary><p>Some programs allow short-term rental income when the property type, market, and rental documentation meet program requirements. Other programs restrict or exclude it.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I refinance a rental property and take cash out?</summary><p>Some DSCR programs offer cash-out refinancing for eligible investment properties. Maximum leverage, seasoning, loan size, credit, reserves, and use-of-proceeds rules vary.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does a higher DSCR guarantee loan approval?</summary><p>No. DSCR is only one part of underwriting. The lender also reviews the borrower or guarantor, property, loan structure, documentation, and applicable program requirements.</p></details>
    </section>


    <section class="llc-section" id="llc-basics">
      <div class="section-label">OWNERSHIP & ENTITY BASICS</div>
      <h2>Should you hold a rental property in an LLC?</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">An LLC can create a separate legal entity for an investment activity and may help separate business operations from personal affairs when it is properly formed and maintained. An LLC does not automatically create tax savings: federal tax treatment depends on the number of owners and any elections made. It is not automatic asset protection or a substitute for appropriate insurance and legal advice.</p>
      <div class="llc-grid">
        <article><h3>Potential advantages</h3><ul><li>Separates business records, contracts, and banking from personal activity</li><li>May help limit an owner's personal liability for certain entity obligations, subject to exceptions and proper operation</li><li>Can provide a structure for multiple owners, succession, and operating agreements</li><li>May make property-level accounting and partner reporting easier</li><li>May offer tax-classification flexibility, depending on ownership and elections</li></ul></article>
        <article><h3>Important trade-offs</h3><ul><li>Formation, annual filings, registered-agent, tax-preparation, and legal costs</li><li>Separate bookkeeping, banking, contracts, and records are essential</li><li>Personal guarantees can still create personal obligations</li><li>Insurance remains important; an LLC does not prevent lawsuits</li><li>Moving an already-financed property into an LLC can raise due-on-sale, title, insurance, tax, and lender-consent issues</li></ul></article>
      </div>
      <div class="llc-steps">
        <h3>Georgia LLC formation: a practical starting checklist</h3>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Choose the ownership plan.</b> Decide who owns the entity, who manages it, and whether one entity or separate property-level entities make sense. Discuss liability, financing, tax, and estate goals with qualified professionals.</li>
          <li><b>Choose a name and Georgia registered agent.</b> The registered agent must have a Georgia street address and be available for official service.</li>
          <li><b>File Articles of Organization.</b> Georgia's online domestic LLC filing currently lists a $110 total filing/service fee. The state advises that online processing time varies.</li>
          <li><b>Create an operating agreement.</b> It is an internal document that should address ownership, contributions, authority, distributions, transfers, and what happens if an owner exits. Have it tailored to the owners and property plan.</li>
          <li><b>Apply for an EIN directly with the IRS.</b> The IRS issues EINs for free. Form the legal entity first; avoid third-party sites that charge just to obtain an EIN.</li>
          <li><b>Open dedicated banking and bookkeeping.</b> Keep rents, deposits, expenses, and owner contributions traceable. Do not mix personal and rental funds.</li>
          <li><b>Check local licensing, zoning, tax, insurance, and lender requirements.</b> These depend on property location, use, ownership, and financing.</li>
          <li><b>Maintain the entity.</b> Georgia LLC annual registration is currently $60 and is due January 1–April 1 each year. Keep the registered agent and business details current.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="fine-print">Georgia fee and deadline snapshot; confirm current requirements before filing. Other states have different fees and rules. A lender may require personal guarantees, specific entity documents, or approval before title is transferred.</p>
        <div class="resource-links">
          <a href="https://ecorp.sos.ga.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Georgia Secretary of State filing portal ↗</a>
          <a href="https://sos.ga.gov/how-to-guide/how-guide-register-domestic-entity" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Georgia LLC formation instructions ↗</a>
          <a href="https://sos.ga.gov/how-to-guide/how-file-annual-registration" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Georgia annual registration ↗</a>
          <a href="https://www.irs.gov/businesses/employer-identification-number" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apply for a free IRS EIN ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="rental-strategy-section" id="rental-strategies">
      <div class="section-label">CHOOSE YOUR RENTAL MODEL</div>
      <h2>Long-term, short-term, or Airbnb-style?</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">The best model depends on local rules, demand, operating capacity, financing terms, and the owner's goals—not just the highest advertised nightly rate.</p>
      <div class="strategy-grid">
        <article><span class="strategy-tag">LONG-TERM</span><h3>Traditional rental</h3><p>Usually a lease measured in months or years, with more predictable rent and fewer guest turnovers.</p><ul><li>Lease, screening, deposits, fair-housing compliance</li><li>Rent collection and renewal process</li><li>Repairs, habitability, inspections, and tenant communication</li><li>Vacancy, nonpayment, and turnover planning</li></ul><b>Best suited to:</b><p>Owners seeking steadier occupancy and a simpler turnover rhythm.</p></article>
        <article><span class="strategy-tag">SHORT-TERM</span><h3>Furnished stays</h3><p>Short stays can produce higher gross revenue in some markets, but income may fluctuate and operations are more intensive.</p><ul><li>Local permits, zoning, occupancy and lodging taxes</li><li>Cleaning, linens, supplies, guest messaging, and check-in</li><li>Seasonality, cancellations, platform fees, and reviews</li><li>More frequent inspections and faster maintenance response</li></ul><b>Best suited to:</b><p>Owners with a strong local demand case and reliable operations.</p></article>
        <article><span class="strategy-tag">PLATFORM HOSTING</span><h3>Airbnb / vacation rental</h3><p>Airbnb is a booking and hosting channel, not a separate legal rental category. Local rules and tax duties still apply to the property and host.</p><ul><li>Verify HOA, lease, zoning, registration, and permit rules</li><li>Confirm who collects and remits each tax or fee</li><li>Set house rules, guest limits, safety and emergency plans</li><li>Check insurance exclusions and platform coverage limits</li></ul><b>Best suited to:</b><p>Owners who can meet local requirements and consistently deliver guest service.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="fine-print">Airbnb does not replace local compliance. In Georgia, the state hotel-motel fee may apply to short stays; marketplace collection rules and local taxes can differ. Confirm the rules for the exact city and county before listing.</p>
      <div class="resource-links">
        <a href="https://www.airbnb.com/help/article/376" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Airbnb: local laws and taxes for hosts ↗</a>
        <a href="https://dor.georgia.gov/state-hotel-motel-faq" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Georgia hotel-motel fee FAQ ↗</a>
        <a href="https://dca.georgia.gov/housing-choice-voucher/landlords/georgia-landlord-tenant-handbook" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Georgia landlord-tenant handbook ↗</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="rental-tools-section" id="rental-tools">
      <div class="section-label">FREE INVESTOR TOOLS</div>
      <h2>Run the numbers before you commit.</h2>
      <div class="tool-grid">
        <article class="tool-card">
          <h3>Long-term rental cash-flow estimator</h3>
          <p>Estimate monthly cash flow after the main operating allowances you enter. This is not DSCR and does not replace a full property analysis.</p>
          <div class="tool-fields">
            <label for="lt-rent">Monthly rent ($)</label><input id="lt-rent" type="number" min="0" value="2200">
            <label for="lt-pitia">Monthly PITIA ($)</label><input id="lt-pitia" type="number" min="0" value="1500">
            <label for="lt-management">Management (% of rent)</label><input id="lt-management" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="8">
            <label for="lt-vacancy">Vacancy reserve (% of rent)</label><input id="lt-vacancy" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="5">
            <label for="lt-maintenance">Maintenance reserve (% of rent)</label><input id="lt-maintenance" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="8">
            <label for="lt-other">Other monthly costs ($)</label><input id="lt-other" type="number" min="0" value="100">
          </div>
          <div class="tool-result"><span>ESTIMATED MONTHLY CASH FLOW</span><strong id="lt-result">$138</strong><small id="lt-detail">Before income taxes, major capital projects, and costs not entered.</small></div>
        </article>
        <article class="tool-card">
          <h3>Short-term rental break-even estimator</h3>
          <p>Estimate monthly gross bookings, operating surplus, and the occupancy needed to cover the costs you enter.</p>
          <div class="tool-fields">
            <label for="st-rate">Average nightly rate ($)</label><input id="st-rate" type="number" min="0" value="180">
            <label for="st-occupancy">Expected occupancy (%)</label><input id="st-occupancy" type="number" min="0" max="100" value="55">
            <label for="st-pitia">Monthly PITIA ($)</label><input id="st-pitia" type="number" min="0" value="1800">
            <label for="st-costs">Other monthly costs ($)</label><input id="st-costs" type="number" min="0" value="900">
          </div>
          <div class="tool-result"><span>ESTIMATED MONTHLY SURPLUS</span><strong id="st-result">$270</strong><small id="st-detail">Estimated monthly gross bookings: $2,970 · Break-even occupancy: 50.0%.</small></div>
          <p class="fine-print">Use a conservative average nightly rate and include cleaning, platform/management fees, utilities, supplies, lodging taxes you must pay, insurance, repairs, and seasonal costs in your expense assumptions. This simple estimate assumes 30 available nights.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="operations-grid">
        <article class="operations-card">
          <h3>Property maintenance planner</h3>
          <p>Use this as a starting checklist; adjust for property age, climate, manufacturer instructions, and local code.</p>
          <div class="checklist">
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Test smoke and carbon-monoxide alarms; review fire extinguishers</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Inspect HVAC filters, condensate drains, and service history</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Check plumbing leaks, water heater, shutoffs, and visible moisture</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Inspect roof, gutters, grading, drainage, and exterior openings</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Check appliances, locks, windows, railings, and trip hazards</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Document work orders, invoices, photos, warranties, and follow-up dates</label>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="operations-card">
          <h3>Rental bookkeeping starter list</h3>
          <p>Track each property separately and keep source documents. Ask a tax professional how each item should be classified.</p>
          <div class="checklist">
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Rent, booking income, cleaning fees, and other receipts</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Mortgage interest, property tax, insurance, and HOA</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Repairs, maintenance, supplies, utilities, and cleaning</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Property management, platform fees, advertising, and software</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Capital improvements tracked separately from routine repairs</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Security deposits, owner contributions, and reserve transfers</label>
            <label><input type="checkbox"> Monthly bank reconciliation and receipt/invoice backup</label>
          </div>
          <p class="fine-print">The IRS expects records supporting rental income and expenses. A tax deduction is not automatic just because a cost appears in this checklist.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/tips-on-rental-real-estate-income-deductions-and-recordkeeping" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS rental recordkeeping guidance ↗</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="management-section">
      <div class="section-label">PROPERTY MANAGEMENT</div>
      <h2>Self-manage or hire a property manager?</h2>
      <div class="management-grid">
        <div><h3>Questions to ask a manager</h3><ul><li>What is the full fee schedule, including leasing, renewal, maintenance coordination, and vacancy fees?</li><li>Who holds security deposits and how are owner funds reported?</li><li>How are vendors selected, insured, and approved?</li><li>What is the emergency response process and after-hours coverage?</li><li>How often are inspections performed and how are photos shared?</li><li>What is the termination process and who owns the tenant/listing records?</li></ul></div>
        <div><h3>Common problems to plan for</h3><ul><li>Late rent or vacancy: written procedures, reserves, and lawful notices</li><li>Water intrusion or mold concerns: prompt response, documentation, and qualified remediation</li><li>HVAC/plumbing failure: vetted emergency vendors and clear spending limits</li><li>Guest complaints or damage: documented check-in/out inspections and response standards</li><li>Unexpected costs: reserve policy and approval thresholds</li><li>Weak bookkeeping: monthly reconciliation and property-level reporting</li></ul></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="faq-section rental-faq">
      <div class="section-label">RENTAL OWNER FAQ</div>
      <h2>Common questions and avoidable mistakes</h2>
      <details><summary>Does an LLC automatically protect my personal assets?</summary><p>No. Protection depends on state law, the facts of a claim, proper entity operation, adequate insurance, and other factors. Personal guarantees, personal wrongdoing, commingling, and certain statutory obligations can create personal exposure. Get legal advice for your circumstances.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I transfer a property with an existing mortgage into my LLC?</summary><p>Do not assume you can. Review the loan documents and obtain advice from the lender, title professional, and attorney before changing ownership. A transfer can affect due-on-sale provisions, title coverage, insurance, taxes, and future refinancing.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I use Airbnb income to qualify for a DSCR loan?</summary><p>Some investor programs accept short-term rental income using approved market-rent reports, historical booking records, or other documentation. Others restrict it or use different calculations. Confirm the exact program before relying on projected nightly income.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Do I need a permit to operate a short-term rental?</summary><p>Possibly. City and county zoning, registration, business licensing, occupancy limits, HOA rules, and tax requirements can apply. Check the exact property jurisdiction before purchasing or advertising.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Is Airbnb's tax collection the end of my tax responsibility?</summary><p>Not necessarily. Platform collection varies by jurisdiction and tax type. Confirm state, county, and city requirements and keep records of what the platform collected versus what remains your responsibility.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What is the difference between a repair and a capital improvement?</summary><p>The tax treatment can differ. Keep invoices and descriptions, and ask your tax professional to classify significant work. Do not automatically treat every renovation or replacement as a current-year repair deduction.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How much should I reserve for maintenance and vacancy?</summary><p>There is no universal percentage. It depends on property age, condition, systems, local vacancy, insurance deductibles, climate, and rental model. Use property-specific estimates and stress-test higher costs and lower occupancy.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What should I do when a tenant reports a serious repair?</summary><p>Respond promptly, follow the lease and applicable law, document the report and work, and use qualified contractors for safety-critical work. Georgia's landlord-tenant handbook explains repair and habitability responsibilities.</p></details>
    </section>
    <section class="closing-cta">
      <div><div class="section-label">YOUR NEXT INVESTMENT STARTS WITH A CONVERSATION</div><h2>Have a property in mind?</h2><p>Share the basics and let's review potential financing paths for your rental investment.</p></div>
      <a class="button primary" href="/apply">Start Your Inquiry <span>→</span></a>
    </section>
    <p class="disclaimer">Educational information only; not a commitment to lend, loan approval, tax advice, legal advice, or investment advice. DSCR examples are estimates. All financing is subject to lender guidelines, underwriting, property eligibility, applicable law, and approval. Program terms and availability may change without notice.</p>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</body>
</html>
